## Source integration — bounded management-fragment RX

`ManagementReassembly.{hpp,cpp}` now runs as a fixed-capacity RX stage in
`WifiEngine::acceptRx` after the caller attests DMA/FCS and channel policy.
It publishes no management payload before contiguous fragments match the
original subtype, sequence number and all three 802.11 addresses. The owner
rejects out-of-order, stale (>500-ms), clock-regressed, overlength and PMF-
protected fragments, and clears pending state on scan replacement, new join,
rejection, radio stop and an invalid RX trust lease. Completed frames are
borrowed only during the synchronous scan/association/link handler. The
standalone `parseFrame` still refuses raw management fragments.

This is a source-only reassembly path included in the normal Xcode target;
it does not authenticate non-PMF management frames, establish physical FCS,
wire RF or prove scan operation. PMF reassembly and negotiation, independent
receive-channel calibration, aggregation, BlockAck and native response timing
remain outstanding. The P2 task stays open pending those integrations.

## Source integration — bounded open-data fragment RX

`DataReassembly.{hpp,cpp}` now joins `WifiEngine::acceptRx` after verified
DMA/FCS and before the existing station address, LLC/SNAP and replay/duplicate
admission. It retains only one ordered, matching unprotected FromDS data/QoS
fragment stream, bounded by a 2342-byte reconstructed MPDU and 500-ms
monotonic deadline. Partial payloads cannot escape. Assembly is reset on
association, scan, radio and trust changes; success consumes one complete
802.11 sequence number at the existing native-payload gate. C++14/C++17
synthetic regressions cover order, identity, QoS, time, overflow, aliasing,
trust revocation and actual open-association delivery.

Each fragment still requires external verified DMA/FCS evidence, which portable
tests cannot supply. The separate native WPA2 CCMP receive owner now verifies
the individual MIC/PN of every fragment before bounded reassembly, binding its
one pending MSDU to the same physical epoch, GTK/PTK key ID and address class.
The completed canonical MPDU alone reaches the existing station/security
admission gate; no partial payload is delivered. This does not complete
BlockAck/A-MPDU reordering, actual hardware receive, association or RF/TPC
permission. Keep the task open.

## Native publication of reassembled open data

The AR9565 `queueReceived` callback formerly decoded the borrowed original
last DMA fragment a second time after `WifiEngine::ReceivedPayload` and thus
discarded every completed fragment stream. The native open-data publication
path now consumes copied source/destination addresses plus a borrowed payload
from the already station-authorized canonical MPDU. It retains epoch, queue,
beacon, controlled-port, live radio, FCS and association guards, and copies
accepted Ethernet bytes synchronously before another RX callback. The native
path explicitly rejects EAPOL here; WPA2 owns its separate protected ingress.
The host fixture confirms that the final raw fragment cannot independently
parse as LLC but its accepted action serializes to the correct Ethernet-II
frame. Native on-device operation still needs independently verified hardware.

## Native-fragment FCS and foreign-BSS isolation

The fragment ingress now checks the selected BSSID, single-DS direction,
valid Ethernet source, and actual station receiver before touching the
association-local reassembly slot. Verified frames from an unrelated AP
therefore cannot preempt a valid in-flight fragment stream. Regression
fixtures also simulate physically retained four-byte FCS on each QoS fragment,
check that no FCS reaches Ethernet, and revoke partial state after an invalid
FCS layout. The hardware owner remains responsible for real FCS evidence.

## Deterministic hostile-MPDU regression

The standalone portable RX fixture now exercises 20,000 seeded malformed
frames under the same C++14/C++17 host sanitizer harness as ordinary
fragmentation. For every emitted frame it verifies the reconstructed bounds,
canonical data type, and absence of fragment bits; rejected and pending
results must revoke the output view. This is a memory/protocol regression,
not a proof that physical DMA/FCS, radio initialization or Tahoe IOKit works.

## Protection-mode isolation at standalone reassembly ingress

The reassembly primitive now rejects protected MPDUs before either a
single-frame fast path or multi-fragment accumulation. This closes a
standalone API gap where direct unfragmented input could previously bypass
the protected-frame rejection; the WifiEngine already used this primitive
only for fragmented open traffic. A regression exercises that explicit denial.

## Native protected QoS A-MSDU receive admission (source-only)

`SimpleATH::receiveNativeProtected` now selects the bounded QoS A-MSDU
receiver only after the normal EDMA/FCS, radio, queue and selected-link gates.
The native pairwise `CcmpSession` must verify the aggregate's MIC/PN and the
A-MSDU selector under **separately negotiated SPP A-MSDU** before any decoded
subframe reaches `WifiEngine::acceptVerifiedProtectedAmsduRx`. That engine
revalidates station/security/port trust and consumes one QoS sequence using
its ordinary per-TID duplicate window. The native mailbox copies each approved
Ethernet-II subframe synchronously before the decrypted scratch is scrubbed.
Wrong trust, malformed aggregates, forbidden EAPOL, duplicate sequences,
invalid association and aggregate fragments fail without delivery. Synthetic
C++14/C++17 ASan+UBSan tests cover the actual CCMP->engine handoff, duplicate
suppression, FCS layout and reset denial.

**Not a live WPA2 A-MSDU path yet:** the native key installer currently keeps
`verifiedSppAmsdu=false`, deliberately refusing all protected aggregates
until authenticated association capabilities and SPP policy are negotiated
and plumbed into the key owner. This change is a safe source integration, not
proof of RF traffic, actual OS packet delivery, SPP negotiation, A-MPDU/BA
reordering or tested Tahoe compatibility. Keep this task open.

## Protected management isolation in the standalone fragment owner

The standalone `ManagementReassembly` now rejects Protected management
MPDUs before its unfragmented fast path, not only when fragment bits are
present. This unprotected/FCS-only owner cannot authenticate PMF and must
not publish even a single protected MPDU or leave stale fragment state.
A synthetic regression confirms denial of an unfragmented Protected beacon,
revocation of a pending cleartext fragment stream, and recovery on a fresh
cleartext management MPDU. The regression fails against the prior source,
and passes under C++14/C++17 ASan+UBSan on the updated implementation.

Actual 802.11w/PMF authenticated management admission is not implemented
by this reassembler; hardware FCS, native RX ownership and radio behavior
remain unverified. The wider MAC task remains open.

## Source-only outbound QoS A-MSDU MPDU composition (2026-11-12)

`DataFrames::makeOpenStationAmsdu` now serializes an unprotected, three-address,
ToDS QoS Data MPDU containing 2–16 Ethernet-II MSDUs. It emits canonical
per-subframe big-endian lengths, RFC 1042 or AARP/IPX bridge-tunnel LLC/SNAP,
zero inter-subframe alignment, and no trailing pad or FCS. Every input and
aggregate bound (including the 2342-byte FCS-free AR9565 MPDU maximum),
station-source identity, TID/sequence and forbidden EAPOL case is checked
before publishing any output bytes. Borrowed input/output/count overlap is
rejected without mutation. The existing RX A-MSDU decoder independently
round-trips the serializer's output in the host regression fixture, including
exact-maximum length, truncation, security rejection and alias cases.
An additional deterministic 256-case sanitizer fixture round-trips mixed
2–16-subframe aggregates with varied TIDs, sequence numbers, lengths,
EtherTypes and inter-subframe padding against the independent RX decoder.

**Not enabled in the native TX path.** The owner still needs to negotiate the
selected AP's A-MSDU capability and maximum length; prove channel/RF/TPC and
MAC aggregation lifecycle/sequence ownership; and explicitly gate OPEN
aggregation against the controlled port. WPA2 requires independently verified
SPP A-MSDU and authenticated encryption with correct QoS/A-MSDU AAD. No source
helper or synthetic success authorizes radio TX or claims live Wi-Fi.
